Text: "Mathematics in its modern formulation is built upon the foundational
       framework of set theory. Every mathematical structure—from the natural
       numbers and real continuum to abstract groups, topological spaces, and
       differentiable manifolds—is formally constructed as a set equipped with
       relations and operations.

       This chapter develops set theory through two complementary stages:

       1. **Naive Set Theory and Core Operations**: The intuitive conception of sets
          as collections of objects, the subset relation, power sets, Boolean
          operations (union, intersection, set difference, symmetric difference),
          arbitrary indexed families, and the foundational discovery of Russell's
          Paradox demonstrating the necessity of formal constraints on comprehension.

       2. **Axiomatic Set Theory (ZFC Foundations)**: The rigorous resolution of
          paradoxes within the formal first-order Zermelo--Fraenkel axiom system
          with the Axiom of Choice, well-founded relations, and the cumulative
          hierarchy."
